fixtures/
tests/
data/
```

## Tests

```bash
pytest -q
```

The test suite covers source normalization, deduplication, correlation, relevance, models, resilience, structured output routing, atomic export, checkpoint semantics, sandbox behavior, fixture research, and security boundary rules.

## Queue and retry behavior

Pending threats survive across cycles with retry metadata. A threat remains eligible while its status is `pending` or `retry`; after three failed attempts it is retained as `quarantined` and stops consuming the per-cycle LLM budget. Deferred threats remain pending and do not block source checkpoint advancement when processing itself is only partial.

The deterministic demo remains independent of provider credentials and is the first validation step after installation.

## Important limitation

A live CVE will not automatically be marked as `REPRODUCED` merely because an LLM describes an exploit. A live research case without a deterministic reproducer remains `INCONCLUSIVE` until actual sandbox observations exist. The deterministic demo is provided to prove the full research/verification contract without relying on network conditions or LLM quotas.


## Configuration

### LLM key
The free-tier default uses Groq. Set `LLM_API_KEY` once with your Groq API key. `LLM_TRIAGE_API_KEY` is optional; when it is blank, SentinelAudit automatically reuses `LLM_API_KEY` for triage. OpenAI and Gemini providers remain supported.

### Research sources
`RESEARCH_SOURCE_URLS` is optional. When it is blank, the application automatically uses the GitHub Security RSS feed as an additional untrusted research source: `https://github.blog/tag/security/feed/`. You can override it with a comma-separated list of RSS, Atom, or JSON feed URLs.

